In article <sb380.728208829@Victoria>, sb380@city.ac.uk (HOLT A D) writes:
> sean@cobra.dra.com writes:
>>What's the difference between printing material that may become out-of-date
>>in a newspaper, and making the material available for anonymous FTP?
> 
> A newspaper is recognised* as having information of current time ("news")
> value only that may become rapidly dated, also the average newspaper reader
> has a fair idea (usually slightly exaggerated) of the likelihood of the
> information in the newspaper being correct [using British examples, the
> Times has a higher reputation for accuracy than the Sunday Sport].
> Too many people, however, think "if it is on a computer it must be right"
> and take no notice of when the file was created (and may not be able to
> find this out anyhow)
> 
> * by "the man in the street"

In my humble opinion anyone who has accessed files using anonymous FTP
knows FTP sites are a hodpodge of various states of accuracy and
timeliness.  Just as some newspapers have a better reputation than others,
some FTP sites have a better reputation than others.  But in any case,
the belief that "the man in the street" may be misled by out-of-date
electronic information doesn't justify the government refusing to release
the information just because the information is in electronic form.

If it is legal for a newspaper to print it, it should be just as legal (and
protected) to distribute it in a FTP site.   Or to put it another way,
PHRACK should have the same rights to print something that the New York
Times has.


Back to the point about the registrar not wanting to release information
in electronic form.

I want my U.S. congresscritter (or MP, or whatever) to send me copies
of proposed legislation in electronic format rather than paper which I
then have to type back in.  Why does the EFF have to have people retype
court documents which were prepared on word processors in the first place?
Yes, it may get out of date, or corrupted by a transmission error, or
any number of things.  These are problems, but not a valid reason for the
government not to release the information in electronic form.

If the government already has the information in electronic form, it should
have the same policies for its release in electronic form as they do for
its release on paper.  For example, if in the past the Berkeley registrar
gave anyone who asked a paper copy of the class schedule, then they should
have the same policy when they put the class schedule in electronic format
and give anyone who asks an electronic copy.
