vidynath@math.ohio-state.edu (Vidhyanath K Rao) writes:


>Vulgarity used with the sole intent of provoking someone would/could be
>construed as fighting words. They have never been protected by the
>first amendment.

I don't know what you are referring to, specifically, but you have some
apparent problems in your general claims.

"Could" is probably a reasonable statement, if one is talking about a
face-to-face confrontation.  "Would" is definitely too strong.


>Any entity that allows its resources to used in this manner, and then
>refuses to take action when such instances have been brought to its
>notice is liable under the law. Show me a case or the specific law that
>disproves this. 
>
>To give a graphic instance: If you insist on calling one particular student,
>say, "wop", and that student does not complain to the university but takes
>legal action, then the university will not be held responsible. But, if the
>student complains to university first, and the university refuses to take
>action, the university is liable. In fact, according to the recent laws on
>providing a workplace free of harassment and intimidation, theuniversity
>may be crimially liable.

Sorry.  First of all, it doesn't sound like you are talking about a
workplace.  Title VII law, with which the "hostile environment" doctrine
is associated, is specifically related to the *workplace* environment.  It
does not apply, for example, to an educational environment. The doctrine also
operates on agency principles.  From UWM Post et al v. University or Wisconsin
System (District Court, E.D. Wisconsin, 1991):

  First, Title VII addresses employment, not educational, settings.  
  Second, even if Title VII governed educational settings, the Meritor 
  holding would not apply to this case.  The Meritor Court held that 
  courts should look to agency principles when determining whether an 
  employer is to be held liable for its employee's actions. [citation 
  omitted]  Since employees may act as their employer's agents, agency 
  law may hold an employer liable for its employees actions.  In 
  contrast, agency theory would generally not hold a school liable for 
  its students' actions since students normally are not agents of the 
  school.


Secondly, it doesn't sound like you are talking about an actionable example
of harassment.  From Meritor Savings Bank v. Vinson (1986):

  ...not all workplace conduct that may be described as "harassment" 
  affects a "term, condition, or privilege" of employment within the 
  meaning of Title VII.  See Rogers v. EEOC, supra, at 238 ("mere 
  utterance of an ethnic or racial epithet which engenders offensive 
  feelings in an employee" would not affect the conditions of employment 
  to sufficiently significant degree to violate Title VII); [citation 
  omitted]  For sexual harassment to be actionable, it must be 
  sufficiently severe or pervasive "to alter the conditions of [the 
  victim's] employment and create an abusive working environment."


